## A B S T R A C T
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are as a category of crystalline porous materials. Extensive interest has been devoted to energy storage and energy conversion applications owing to their unique advantages of periodic architecture, high specific surface area, high adsorption, high conductivity, high specific capacitance, and high porosity. One-dimensional (1D) nanostructures have unique surface effects, easily regulated size, good aggluti­ nation of the substrate, and other distinct properties amenable to the field of energy storage and conversion. Therefore, 1D nanostructures could further improve the characteristic properties of MOFs, and it is of great importance for practical applications to control the size and morphological characteristics of MOFs. The elec­ trochemical application of 1D MOFs is mainly discussed in this review, including energy storage applications in supercapacitors and batteries and energy conversion applications in catalysis. In addition, various synthesis strategies for 1D MOFs and their architectures are presented.
## 1. Introduction
Over the years, with the rapid development in science and technol­ ogy, increasing shortage of energy resources, and increasingly severe environmental contamination, the global energy demand has been growing. One of the most efficient measures to deal with environmental problems and energy requirements is to develop efficient and clean renewable energy sources [1–3]. Recently, considerable efforts have been directed toward developing diverse rechargeable and convertible energy storage and conversion devices, such as supercapacitors (SCs) [4], full cells, and rechargeable batteries such as Li-ion batteries (LIBs), Li–sulfur batteries (LSBs), Zn–air batteries, and Na-ion batteries (SIBs) [5–11]. These devices are extremely promising owing to their high storage efficiency and energy conversion, which can alleviate the intermittent distribution of energy in time and space as well as the impact on the environment. Fundamentally, these electrochemical de­ vices or systems operate on the basis of diverse energy-conversion re­ actions, such as the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [12,13], oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), and hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) [14], which provide energy by directly transforming chemical energy (e.g., zinc, methanol, ethanol, and hydrogen) into electrical energy and store it in the reverse process [11,15–25]. The intrinsic kinetics of these re­ actions emphasize the pivotal role of electrocatalysis and photocatalysis
[26].
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which are known as porous co­ ordination polymers (PCPs), are a burgeoning class of porous materials. MOFs are metal–organic backbone materials with periodic reticular structures formed by the self-assembly of organic ligands and metal ions and are also known as metal–organic coordination polymers or inorga­ nic–organic hybrid materials [27,28]. MOFs exhibit high crystallinity, high porosity, strong metal–ligand interactions, special periodic archi­ tecture, high specific surface area, and high adsorption; owing to these properties, MOFs have recently become one of the most attractive ma­ terials for both engineers and scientists and have been widely designed, prepared, and applied [29–31]. Over the past several years, MOFs have been explored as precursors for synthesizing diverse nanomaterials with orderly porous morphological features—from carbon-based materials to metal-based materials [32]. Extensive research has been carried out not only for essential interests such as energy transfer and capture of in­ termediates in catalysis but also for promising practical applications including gas separation and storage, heterogeneous catalysis, biomed­ ical applications, chemical sensing, and proton conduction. The distinctive advantages of MOFs, including crystalline porous architec­ ture, adjustable pore diameter, highly dispersed metal components, high porosity, and high-ratio surface area, allow them to have excellent performance in various applications [33], such as energy storage [34–36], drug transportation [37,38], and catalysis [39,40].
With the development of nanotechnology, nanoarchitectures have been the focus of many research efforts, especially in the field of elec­ trochemical energy storage (EES) [41–43]. Previous studies have shown that the size influence of the nanoarchitectures improves the kinetics and thermodynamics of the electrochemical reactions to some extent. 1D nanostructures offer direct current pathways and compress the ion pervasion distance, supplying a fast pervasion pathway for electron/ mass transport. 1D architectures also have the advantages of increasing the electrolyte–electrode contact area and decreasing the charge/ discharge time. Furthermore, they can guarantee structural stability during charge/discharge processes [44–46].
Different types of MOFs have been prepared and applied in catalytic reactions, hydrogen storage, sensors, gas adsorption and separation, drug release, and other fields. With the increase in MOF substances and the development of composite MOF materials, MOFs will have numerous application prospects. In catalysis applications, compound MOFs constructed using different metal mixtures will further improve the catalytic efficiency. In storage applications, MOFs have an extraor­ dinary cycling lifetime and preeminently fast charge/discharge abilities [11,47]. Accordingly, the growth of multifunctional MOFs and com­ posite MOF substances will significantly advance their application fields and extremely advance the mutual growth of disciplines.
Previous reviews have provided excellent summaries of the electro­ chemical applications of MOFs [40,48–54]. However, in terms of sci­ entific and technological developments in MOFs, it is necessary to provide an up-to-date review. Therefore, in this review, we systemati­ cally summarize the synthetic methods, morphology characterization, and electrochemical properties of 1D MOFs. The research progress of 1D MOFs in catalysis, batteries, and SCs is summarized to facilitate future breakthroughs. As shown in Fig. 1, this review follows the same format as other reviews, starting with an introduction to the functions and applications of MOFs as well as the general content of this paper, and then proceeding to the main topic, which is divided into the following parts: first, the synthesis of 1D MOFs; second, the structure of 1D MOFs; third, electrochemical applications of 1D MOFs; and finally, the conclusion and outlook of the article.
## 2. Preparation of 1D MOFs
MOFs, as a category of crystalline porous materials, have attracted the attention of many researchers for their potential applications in environmental remediation and sustainable energy. They are assembled by organic ligands and inorganic vertices (metal clusters or ions) (Fig. 2). Nevertheless, the diversification and functionalization of MOFs remains a challenge; however, they are necessary for the development of highly active MOF-based substances. As mentioned in the introduction, because of the important role of MOF membranes, their preparation methods are also varied [45]. There are many methods to synthesize MOFs, and many new methods have been developed in recent years. A few of these are introduced in this section.
## 2.1. Recrystallization method
The first method that can be used to prepare 1D MOFs is recrystal­ lization. Nanotubes have generated significant interest, and the prepa­ ration of the first mono-crystal MOF nanotubes is reported. Xu et al. succeeded in synthesizing overlength mono-crystalline Co-MOF nano­ tubes that reached a length of $20{-}35~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and a diameter of ${\approx}70~\mathrm{nm}$ ; these nanotubes can also have parallel multichannels (window size: 1.1 nm) if an amorphous MOF-mediated recrystallization method is used (Fig. 3a). The synthesized MOF nanotubes can function as nanometersized columns to separate the macromolecules. When placed in an argon atmosphere, the carbonization of Co-MOF nanotubes leads to the formation of long, 1D carbon nanofibers (CNFs). The carbonization of the dicyandiamide with Co-MOF nanotubes as a secondary carbon source in the presence of nitrogen forms a layered structure composed of CNFs wrapped by carbon nanotubes that reach a length of $200{-}300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and diameter of $20{-}30~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ; cobalt nanoparticles decorate the top of the structure. Hierarchical dendrites, which are generated by the recrys­ tallization method, contain carbon nanofiber trunks and nanotube branches and have been used in rechargeable Zn–air batteries and exhibit excellent electrocatalytic activity for oxygen reduction. This work provides a novel strategy to prepare MOF nanotubes and correla­ tive 1D nanoarchitectures [55].
Furthermore, the bidirectional diffusion of components positioned the recrystallization on the parent crystalline surface in space, contrib­ uting to hollow MOF architectures. Interestingly, hollow architectures are able to be formed by dissolution and recrystallisation processes during the phase transformation process. In particular, in one-pot fabrication, the initially kinetically fast-forming unstable phase MOF will eventually transform into a more thermally stable phase. Dissolu­ tion of the unstable MOF and recrystallisation of the stable MOF will contribute to c the final stable MOF phase [56].
## 2.2. Hydrothermal synthesis
The second method used to prepare MOF films is hydrothermal synthesis, which is the synthesis of substances by means of chemical reactions in aqueous solutions at temperatures of $100-1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and pressures of $1\ \mathrm{MPa}\sim1\$ GPa. Under supercritical and subcritical hy­ drothermal conditions, the hydrothermal reaction takes place in certain high-temperature solid-phase reactions at the molecular level, resulting in improved reactivity (Fig. 3b-c). The homogeneous and heterogeneous nucleation mechanisms of the hydrothermal reactions differ from the diffusion mechanisms of the solid-phase reactions, and they can produce new materials and compounds that cannot be synthesized by other methods. The development of a range of mild, high-temperature, and high-pressure hydrothermal reactions and hydrothermal synthesis routes on the basis of these reactions has become an important way to obtain most inorganic functional materials and inorganic compounds with special compositions and structures [57].
Scientists who work in the field of electrocatalytic material chemis­ try have shown increasing interest in MOFs over the past few years. Ultrathin $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{N i}$ thermometal organic framework nanobelts were pro­ duced using the hydrothermal technique and can be directly used as high-efficiency electrocatalysts [58]. A facile Ni-based MOF, formulated as $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}.12\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , was constructed as a pseudocapacitive material for SCs through a hydrothermal reaction [59]. 1D $Z\mathbf{n}$ -BTC and Co-BTC nanowires were built to construct 1D ZIF-based nanostructures. The $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC nanowires were transformed into double-shelled ZIF-8 nano­ tubes in a 1.2 M Hmim water/ethanol admixture $(\mathrm{V_{ethanol}{:}V_{w a t e r}=9:1})$ ) at $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . MOFs, because of their unique porous structure and promising high performance, can be used directly in SCs. A mixed so­ lution of water and dimethylformamide (DMF) was used to fabricate NiMOF by a one-step facile hydrothermal process [60].
Furthermore, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Bipyridine (4,4’-Bpy), $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , and thiophene-2,5- dicarboxylate (Tdc) were used as raw materials to synthesize S,Ncontaining $\mathrm{Mn^{II}~[(T d c)(4,4^{\cdot}{-}B p y)]_{n}}$ with diverse morphological charac­ teristics via a hydrothermal method. In addition, the influences of different anions ${(\mathsf{S O}_{4}}^{2-}$ $,\mathsf{C l}^{-},\mathsf{N C}$ ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O^{-}})$ and hydrothermal reaction times (0, 2, and $4\mathrm{h}$ ) on the composition, morphology, and ORR activity of the synthesized Mn-MOFs were extensively studied. The re­ sults show that the reaction of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Bpy and $\mathrm{MnSO}_{4}$ with Tdc for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ can result in the formation of relatively homogeneous 1D MOF nanorods [61]. Also, 4,4’-Biphenyl dicarboxylic acid (BPDC) was used as a ligand to obtain Ni/Co-based bimetallic organic frameworks (Ni/Co-MOF) by hydrothermal synthesis. Architectural characterizations indicate that the Ni/Co-MOF is highly crystalline and consists of ordered 3D cuboids assembled from 1D micro-nanorods with cobalt and nickel ions coordinated to the BPDC ligands. Furthermore, Ni/Co-MOF remained stable in air up to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [62].
According to the studies, it is shown that there are lots of factors affecting the hydrothermal reaction, such as temperature, pressure, pH, reactant concentration and so on. Hydrothermal synthesis of nano­ materials offers high purity, good crystal development, and narrow particle size distribution, and they avoid impurities and structural de­ fects caused by post-treatment such as high temperature calcination or ball milling. It also allow the preparation of single crystals, thin films and nanoparticles of different morphological characteristics. Due to the particularity of the reaction conditions, hydrothermal treatment also has the challenges. The process of crystal growth and material synthesis cannot be seen. And it has high equipment requirements and high costs, and is less suitable for batch production.
## 2.3. Template approach
The third method for preparing the MOF films is the template approach. Template-directed synthesis of MOFs is of increasing interest; it involves depositing materials with a nanostructure, shaping the tem­ plate, and then removing the template through physical or chemical methods to obtain nanomaterials with the template standard morpho­ logical characteristics and size. As an effective method for the prepa­ ration of nanomaterials, the template method is governed by the fact that the chemical reactions, whether in the liquid or gas phase, take place in a controlled area, which distinguishes this method from the ordinary methods. The template method for synthesizing nanomaterials has many advantages compared with direct synthesis: the template can be used as a carrier to precisely control the size, morphology, archi­ tecture, and performance of the nanomaterials; the fabrication and as­ sembly of nanomaterials can be integrated; the problem of dispersion stability of nanomaterials can be solved; the synthesis process is rela­ tively uncomplicated; and a range of sizes are suitable for mass production.
New mechanisms of growth controlled by 1D MOF superstructures and nanostructures under surface steering effects and size limits have been found. It was discovered that consistent polycrystalline nanite hollow nanotubes and solid nanorods were formed in $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ pellicle pores via interfacial synthesis templated by the orbital-engraved poly­ carbonate (PCTE) films. Nevertheless, single-crystalline ZIF-8 nanowires grew within $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ holes, and all primarily exhibited a crystal orienta­ tion with the $\{100\}$ plane parallel to the long axis of the pole and possessed large aspect ratios of up to 60 (Fig. 3d–f). The findings offer a generalizable method for determining the size, lattice orientation, and morphology of the MOF nanomaterials [63]. Scientists have paid great attention to 1D carbon nanomaterials owing to their remarkable me­ chanical, thermal, and electrical potential, which could potentially contribute to many important applications. However, the synthetic processes used to fabricate these materials can be very complicated and consume a great deal of energy; thus, the principal goal is to develop facile and effective approaches to manufacture them. A catalyst-free and self-templated strategy for the preparation of 1D carbon nanorods by means of a thermal transition of morphologically preserved, rod-shaped MOFs has been proposed. The non-hollow (solid) as-synthesized carbon nanorods can be transmuted into two- to six-layered graphene nanobelts by sonochemical adjustment followed by chemical activation. The properties of these graphene nanoribbons and MOF-derived carbon nanorods in supercapacitor electrodes indicate that the synthetic process results in materials that are functionally available. In addition, this method is easily extendable and can be applied to produce graphene nanobelts and carbon nanorods on the industrial level [64].
The template approach aids MOF structuring via specific in­ terconversions with MOF precursor organic ligands or metal ions. Another major example of the template approach is the inhibition or enhancement of crystal growth in the desired direction via using mol­ ecules that specifically interconvert with MOF precursors, contributing to anisotropic growth of MOF crystals. This approach is particularly suitable for the construction of 1D architectures [65]. It finds that va­ riety of experimental parameters, containing the concentration of the reactants, the pore size of the template and the type of metal salt, had obvious impacts on the morphological characteristic of the products. In general, the template approach can realize well-controlled architectures of 1D MOFs. The controlled conversion processes and the properly designed templates allow the morphological characteristic and compo­ sition of the 1D MOFs to be adjusted. It is also doable to adapt the length and diameter of the obtained 1D MOFs by means of selecting appro­ priate templates. In general, the template synthesis is effective in con­ trolling the morphological characteristics of MOFs and has been widely used to fabricate diverse 1D MOFs. Despite its broad applicability, the tedious process of preparing templates and the subsequent removal step hinders its large-scale application [66]. Appropriate mold removal conditions are essential to retain the intrinsic architecture of the MOF due to the relatively high sensitivity of MOFs to strict conditions. In addition, the template surface, to achieve high-precision spatial locali­ zation of the MOF precursor, should be pretreated, thus guaranteeing strong interaction between the MOF precursor and the template. This will effectually prevent the respective growth of MOF crystals on the template surface and in solution [56].
## 2.4. Solvothermal process
The fourth method for preparing MOF films is the solvothermal process, which is carried out in a solvent with the aid of a specific chemical reaction under certain temperature and pressure conditions. The synthesis reaction is generally carried out in an airtight container or autoclave and occurs under subcritical or supercritical conditions. At this time, the reactivity of water or other solvents is increased, and the chemical and physical properties of the substance in the solvent are greatly altered, facilitating the formation of sub-stable substances with novel structures.
As shown in Fig. 3g, Jiang et al. recently designed a multifunctional MOF-based heterogeneous architecture consisting of magnetic nano­ sphere embellishment and 1D MIL-53(Fe) microrods (MHMCs), which can be easily synthesized by a contrivable one-pot solvothermal reaction method [67,68]. Various Ni/Fe-based trimetallic MOFs have been syn­ thesized by a solvothermal method for direct use as high-efficiency electrocatalysts [69]. For example, Ni/Co-MOFs were synthesized by a facile one-step solvothermal process that requires the assistance of mi­ crowaves instead of interface metal-cation interchange on the pre­ fabricated monometallic MOF mold, resulting in an extremely homogeneous distribution of the two species as well as high architec­ tural integrity [70]. Zhang et al. also successfully fabricated a Co-based MOF via a solvothermal process and characterized it by elemental analysis, single-crystal X-ray diffraction (XRD), infrared (IR) spectros­ copy, and powder XRD [71].
Solvothermal process has many advantages. Firstly, it has high reactivity. In this method, the solvent is at a temperature and pressure above its critical point, which allows most substances to be dissolved, thus allowing reactions that cannot occur under conventional conditions to take place. The solvothermal synthesis method has the potential to replace solid phase reactions as well as difficult synthetic reactions due to the change in the nature of the reaction and the increased activity of the reactants. Secondly, special structures and values of substances play important roles. Intermediate and medium-steady states and special phases are easily produced. As a result, synthetic products with special medium-steady structures and special condensed states can be synthe­ sized. Low melting point compounds, substances with high steam pres­ sure and which cannot be generated in the melt, and high temperature decomposition phases can be generated by crystallizing at low temper­ atures of solvent heat. Thirdly, it is easy to fine-tune the reaction tem­ perature, time, and other factors. Fourthly, it has the advantage of good crystallinity. The solvent also acts in that it can control the growth of the crystals during the reaction. It has been shown that the use of different solvents produces different forms of products. The low temperature and isobaric solution conditions of solvent heat favor the growth of crystals with few defects, good orientation, high perfection, high crystallinity of the synthesized product and easy control of the particle size of the product crystals. Fifthly, it has a superiority with good controllability. The ambient atmosphere is easily adjustable under solvent heat condi­ tions, which facilitates the production of low valence intermediate compounds and compounds of special valence that can be homoge­ neously doped. The approach also has the advantages of low energy consumption, low agglomeration and controllable particle shape. Nevertheless, this process also has some challenges. The reaction system is in a closed vessel, thus it is not easy to check the reaction intuitively. Meanwhile, it is difficult to study the reaction mechanism because only the results can be seen. Also the yield is low, the purity of the product is not high, as well as the size and shape of the product is not ideal.
## 2.5. Other synthesis methods
In addition to the aforementioned approaches to prepare 1D MOFs, there are many others, some of which will be described in this section. The well-defined 1D nanoarchitectures of MOFs are difficult to acquire, particularly nanowires with long lengths and small diameters. Xiang et al. reported a method for the rapid construction of MOF nanowires in alcohol at room temperature and under shuttering-free conditions. The crystallized MOF nanowires had a diameter of ${\approx}25~\mathrm{nm}$ and a length of ${\approx}5~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ and demonstrated a high catalytic activity for the selective oxidation of benzoic alcohol under mild conditions. The mechanism and preparation process of the MOF nanowires were examined in situ using $^1\mathrm{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy, Fourier-transform IR spectroscopy, and fluorescent probe technology. It was discovered that the alcohol molecules guide the assembly of MOF nanowires by hydrogen bonding with organic ligands [72]. A microfluidic strategy based on a novel nanoliter droplet was used for an ultrafast and continuous synthesis of the MOF crystals, and the hetero-architecture was reported. Typical MOF architectures, such as IRMOF-3, HKUST-1, UiO-66, and MOF-5, were prepared within a few minutes by sol­ vothermal methods with much faster reaction kinetics compared to the traditional batch processes. This method was successfully extended to the synthesis of an exacting $\mathrm{Ru}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}_{2}$ architecture requiring highpressure hydrothermal synthesis. Ultimately, three different types of core–shell MOF composites ( $\mathsf{M O F}{-}5@\mathrm{diCH}_{3}$ -MOF-5, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ , and $\mathrm{Co_{3}B T C_{2}@\ N i_{3}B T C_{2})}$ were synthesized in continuous-flow mode using a distinctive two-step integrated microfluidic synthesis strategy. Compared to bare MOF-5, the composites exhibited better architectural stability under moist conditions, and the catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ was enhanced. The microfluidic process allowed the sequential preparation of high-quality MOF crystals in a time-efficient manner, giving rise to unique morphologies and representing a possible alternative to the multistep and time-consuming MOF synthesis methods [73].
In recent years, a new method to prepare MOFs using chemical vapor deposition (CVD) is presented. Nevertheless, the existing MOF-CVD approach is restricted to the use of gaseous organic precursors, which hinders the application of the CVD process to an extensive range of promising organic linkers. Liu et al. used a dual-temperature-zone CVDassisted method to enable the in-situ growth of an array of conductive $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ nanowire arrays interfaced between a solid organic pre­ cursor and solid Cu foil, which overcomes both the low decomposition temperatures of MOFs and high sublimation temperatures of organic ligands. In this process, oxidized water $\left(0\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ is the key to acquiring crystalline $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ nanowire arrays and is regarded as a funda­ mental development mode. A symmetrical supercapacitor with $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ nanowire arrays used as a substrate has a normalized electric capacity of $41.1\upmu\mathrm{F}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and high specific surface area of $0.5\mathrm{A}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ , which is 2–5-fold higher than that of most carbon materials. This study shows that the synthesis strategy can be extended from gas- or liquid-based reactions to solid–solid reactions and has potential in the manufacture of a wide range of conducting MOFs and immediate supercapacitor applications [74]. The microemulsion method is also an effective strategy to adjust and control the growth of MOFs in a partic­ ular direction, similar to other methods. Symmetrical MOF nanorods with a length of $100{-}125~\mathrm{nm}$ and particle size of approximately $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ were successfully prepared in a water–oil microemulsion system. Evidently, the molar ratio of surfactant to water influences the morphological characteristics and dimensions of the nanorods [75].
## 3. Architecture of 1D MOFs
Metal ions coordinated with organic ligands to form crystalline framework materials, which are known as MOFs, are a novel category of porous substances that have emerged in the recent years. In addition to studying the architecture of MOFs on the molecular scale, scientists have recently made significant progress in producing more complicated mesoscopic/macroscopic scale architectures, in which MOF nano­ crystals serve as blocks to fabricate higher-order super-architectures. This allows the opportunity to develop the material properties by tailoring the physical form of MOFs instead of altering the chemical composition [65,78]. 1D nanomaterials are extensively researched with the goal to achieve novel morphologies and multiple functionalities with enhanced properties and applications. As mentioned in this section, 1D nanomaterials can be fabricated using gas- and liquid-phase technolo­ gies and solvothermal and hydrothermal syntheses, among other ap­ proaches, and they are potential candidates for energy storage devices, including SCs and batteries. In addition, multifarious 1D architectures, such as porous, hollow, shelled, and other complex 1D nanostructures, generally have more robust electrochemical properties than the simple 1D construction. Accordingly, these intricate and novel 1D MOF archi­ tectures are reviewed in this section [79–81].
## 3.1. Simple 1D structures
A 1D MOF has a unique structural morphology. These MOFs are mainly constructed on the nanoscale and can be micron-sized in length and as wide as possible to maintain the width in nano units. There are generally two ways to distinguish the dimensions: two dimensions are below $100\mathrm{nm}$ , and one dimension above $100\mathrm{nm}$ is 1D; the topography is characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and trans­ mission electron microscopy (TEM).
1D nanostructures include tubes, rods, wires, needles, fibers, hooks and helices, which are characterized through a high ratio of one dimension to the other two that are equivalent and are in the nanoscale (length $\gg$ width or diameter). In the literature, many of these are acicular-like architectures that can even reach the millimeter range in length. Rods with an aspect ratio of 3–5 may be aggregates of individual building blocks such as polyhedral, dots or plates. Wires have lengthwidth ratios of even more than 1000, and they are more flexible and slimmer than rods. The architecture of the wire is very simple, with no room for any defects, so that electrons pass through unhindered. Compared to rods and wires, the architectures of tubes are more com­ plex, and tubes can be single-walled or multi-walled. Their properties depend on how they are rolled up. Strips are extremely thin, flat ar­ chitectures with a ribbon-like shape and a rectangular cross-section. In addition, they have a high specific surface area [82–84]. The design of the 1D part is the easiest; nanorods, [85] nanoribbons, nanotubes, and nanowires, all of which are slender, are shown in Fig. 3a–c.
## 3.2. Porous structures
MOFs have also attracted increasing attention in the field of clean energy owing to their permanent porosity, high surface area, and controllable architecture (Fig. 4d–e) [76]. A new porous, tubular 1DMOF, which was fabricated from $p,p^{\prime}$ -diphenyl-diphosphate and 1,2- bis(4-pyridyl) ethane, and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , was constructed by a straightfor­ ward self-assembly method in either nanorod form or as needle-like microcrystals depending on the synthesis conditions [86]. The synthe­ sis of MIL-110 (Al) NTs was reported by solvothermal process. This MOF presents a 1D porous architecture consisting of hexagonal channels with a diameter of $1.6\mathrm{nm}$ [87]. The MOF NWs are 1D nanoarchitectures with diameters in the nanometer range and lengths up to a few centimeters. Their length-diameter ratio is highly variable and evidently higher compared to that of NRs. It is important to note that several NWs exhibiting 1D porous structures with MOFs have been reported. Since the channels of MOFs with diameters of up to a few nanometers are parallel to the main axis of the NW, these 1D nanostructures are also considered to be MOF NTs [55].
## 3.3. Hollow structures
The sectional image shows that ZIF-8-NPCs, which are located in the NPCF (shown in Fig. 4f–g), have a hollow architecture. TEM was used to characterize the NPCFs to clearly observe the hollow architectures. As shown in Fig. 4h, the symmetrical hollow cubic architecture derived from ZIF-8 is apparent [88,89].
## 3.4. Shelled structures
Interestingly, the thickness of the outer and inner walls of the doubleshelled ZnCo-ZIF nanotubes were almost parallel when a small amount of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ was introduced (Fig. 4i). As the content of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ increased, phase abruption could not occur, or the ligand transformation was blocked by the thick outer wall; the low molar ratio of $\mathrm{mim}^{-}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ could not crystallize the secondary ZIF-8 shell layer, resulting in the formation of single-shelled ZnCo-ZIF nanotubes (shown in Fig. 4j-k). Intriguingly, the tips of some nanotubes showed a tendency to be triple-shelled [90].
## 3.5. Other complex structures
To date, few reports on complex and diverse 1D MOF nano­ architectures have been published, owing to the small radii and convex surface of curvature that are detrimental to subsequent heterogeneous nucleation and interface construction during growth. The direct trans­ formation from mother-type MOFs into daughter-type MOFs has been demonstrated, which effectively solves this problem. 1D M-BTC nano­ wires were used to obtain 1D ZIF-based nanostructures. The $Z\mathbf{n}$ -BTC nanowires were transformed into double-shelled ZIF-8 nanotubes under the condition of a $1.2\mathrm{M}$ Hmim water/ethanol mixture $\mathrm{(V_{ethanol}}$ : $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{water}}=9{:}1\rangle$ at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . These constructed 1D nanowire–nanotube and double-shelled nanotube MOF architectures also lack inorganic crystals and cannot be constructed using a conventional method. In the same way, the use of the more stable ZnCo-MOF-74 as the mother MOF yielded core–shell hybridization and single-shelled nanotube architec­ tures with distinctly granular surfaces and a larger particle size of ZnCoZIF owing to the high Co content of the mother MOF, while an excess amount of the mother MOF resulted in morphological control (Fig. 4l–o).
## 4. 1D MOFs for EES applications
MOFs, as an evolving classes of functional materials, are used for energy storage and conversion storage. The versatility, controllable chemical compositions, high porosity, and diverse structures of MOFs offer enormous opportunities for the design of electrode materials for rechargeable batteries. Although MOFs have these advantages, they face various challenges as electrode materials that hinder their practical applications. From this perspective, the challenges and opportunities of MOF-derived materials and MOFs/MOF composites for rechargeable batteries such as LIBs, LSBs, lithium– ${{\mathrm{.0}}_{2}}$ batteries, Zn–air batteries, and other battery types are presented [11,40,44,52,91–94].
MOF-derived nanoporous carbons (NPCs) have been considered as potential electrode materials for conversion devices and energy storage. Nevertheless, MOF-derived NPCs usually have poor electrical conduc­ tivity due to the lack of connectedness between the microporedominated storage mechanism and these particles, which block elec­ tron- and mass-transfer and result in poor electrochemical capability. For the past few years, 1D-MOF-derived carbon nanostructures prepared using an electrospinning approach have become potential materials for both energy conversion applications and EES. Recent advances have been made in the development of 1D hollow or porous MOF-derived CNFs, which are prepared via the electrospinning approach, and their use in energy storage devices, such as SCs, rechargeable batteries, and conversion devices. We extensively discuss the formation mechanism, prefabrication process, and architecture–activity relationship of hollow or porous CNFs. Then, we discuss prospects for the expansion of elec­ trostatically spun MOF-derived carbon nanomaterials for conversion applications and energy storage applications. Guidelines for preparing 1D hollow or porous carbon nanomaterials from MOFs that use elec­ trospinning technology are also provided [35,95–97].
## 4.1. SCs
SCs have gained increasing interest as a novel class of energy storage devices. Compared with conventional rechargeable batteries, SCs have higher power densities, faster discharge/charge rates, and longer cycle lives (Fig. 6b). MOFs are promising electrodes for SCs owing to their high surface areas and regular pore dimensions. MOFs can be directly employed as new electrode materials because of their unique architec­ ture combined with their pseudocapacitive redox centers. The direct application of MOFs as SC electrodes has been less researched because of the spatial site resistance of ion insertion due to the incompatibility of MOFs with the electrolyte, as well as their pore dimensions, and still faces many challenges (Table 1). Nevertheless, the extensive application of MOFs which are used as electrode materials is still restricted via their instability in the electrolyte and relatively low conductivity, which are able to be partially compensated by means of structural optimization. Therefore, reducing the size of MOFs to the micro/nanoscale and using them directly as electrodes has received considerable attention in the field of electrochemistry [45]. Recently, micro/nanoscale MOFs have indicated great potential in this application by means of their control­ lable pores, high specific surface area and diverse metal species. Meanwhile, they are used as electrode materials for solid state SCs owing to their full exploitation of high porosity and good electrical conduc­ tivity, and have exhibited excellent capacitive properties [98,99].
A mixture of water and DMF, rather than pure DMF, was used to fabricate an Ni-MOF by a one-step, facile hydrothermal process. Char­ acterization revealed that the Ni-MOF possessed a sparsely stacked, packed layer-cube architecture with an affluent mesoporous structure, which is favorable for ion transport and charge transfer in SCs. The NiMOF employed as a working electrode in an electrolyte solution of $2\mathrm{{M}}$ KOH exhibited an extraordinary specific capacitance of $804\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ at 1 A g−1 , a remarkable rate capacitance of $534\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , and $302\mathrm{F}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ retention after 5000 cycles in this three-electrode system (Fig. 5a–b). Conductive MOFs are a novel type of potential electrode material for microporous SCs. The work of Xu et al. suggested that the application of Cu–CAT in a high-property solid-state supercapacitor is highly feasible (Fig. 5c). The Ni-MOF//AC asymmetrical SC device was assembled with active carbon and Ni-MOF as negative and positive electrode materials, respectively, to further investigate the practical application of the NiMOF. The SC demonstrated a superior specific energy of 31.5 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a specific power of $800~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}$ . These results indicate that NiMOFs are a class of potential pole materials for high-property SCs (Fig. 5d–e) [60]. Another conductive MOF materials with highly ori­ ented NWAs were successfully synthesized and used as free electrodes with conductive binders and additives. This basic solid-state supercapacitor was fabricated, and it exhibited a very high surface capacitance of ${\approx}22~\upmu\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is above the previously reported values for other most porous carbon-based symmetric solid-state SCs, such as activated carbon, carbon nanotubes, and MOF-based SCs (Fig. 5f). Most importantly, their work shows that the electrochemical performance of MOF materials (e.g., capacitance and rate performance) can be enhanced by engineering their morphological characteristics from irregular crystals to highly oriented NWAs and taking full advan­ tage of their excellent conductivity and high porosity [100].
Due to their superior cyclability when connected to batteries as well as their high power density, electrochemical double-layer capacitors (EDLCs) have become an important technology for electrical energy storage and could perform a significant role in the large-scale deploy­ ment of smart power grids, electric vehicles, and intermittent renewable energy sources. Owing to the charge–discharge rates and the capaci­ tance of EDLCs scale with high electrical conductivity and surface area, respectively, porous carbon nanotubes, activated carbon, and holey or crosslinked graphene exclusively serve as the active electrode materials in EDLCs. With their specific surface areas far surpassing those of active carbons, MOFs which have the potential to challenge the exclusive use of carbon electrodes in EDLCs. Nevertheless, the high porosity of MOFs is usually coupled with extremely poor electrical conductivity, which has drastically prevented them from serving as active electrodes in EDLCs. It was demonstrated that $\mathrm{{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}}$ , an MOF with high electrical con­ ductivity, can be employed as the bottom electrode material in EDLCs (Fig. 5g–h). For the first time, an SC was prepared from pure MOFs as the activated materials, without conductive additive agent or other adhe­ sives. The area capacitance of the MOF-based device outperformed most of those based on the carbon materials, and the capacity retention sur­ passed $90\%$ in 10,000 cycles, which is consistent with commercial-grade equipment. Considering the compositional and configurable adjust­ ability of MOFs, these outcomes preview the arrival of a novel genera­ tion of SCs with activated electrode materials that can be rationally tuned at the molecular level [34]. Electrochemical measurements show that the activation of galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) at a high current density can significantly improve the capacity of Ni/Co-MOF by decreasing the ion penetration shock and charge transfer resistance. The specific capacitance of Ni/Co-MOF increases from $156.5\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ to $990.7\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , and the coulombic efficiency after CV–GCD activation reaches $77.6\%$ to $98.0\%$ . (CV is cyclic voltammetry.) The activated $\mathbf{Ni}/\mathbf{\Lambda}$ Co-MOF exhibited significant pseudocapacitive behavior with high specific capacitances at diverse current densities and good cycling sta­ bility after 1000 cycles. The capacity of Ni/Co-MOF can reach up to $405.0\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ when the current density is $20\mathrm{A}\ g^{-1}$ , remaining at $553.2\mathrm{F}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ after 3000 circles at a current density of 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Compared with monometallic Co-MOF and Ni-MOF, bimetallic Ni/Co-MOF has a higher capacity at diverse current densities because of the lower internal resistance, ion diffusion impendence, and charge transfer resistance [62].
Apart from Cu-CAT, a new N-doped MOF-based supercapacitor graded carbon fiber (NPCF) was constructed using the thermal cracking method. Because of its distinct 1D hollow architecture, NPCF shows better energy storage capacity compared to other MOF-derived carbon substances [88]. An electroactive Ni-based MOF, formulated as $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(b t c)_{2}.12H_{2}O}$ , was prepared through a hydrothermal reaction. In KOH aqueous electrolyte, the fabricated MOF exhibited excellent pseudocapacitive behavior, with a specific capacitance of $726\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ . It also exhibited superior electrochemical stability, retaining $94.6\%$ of its initial capacitance after consecutive 1000 cycles. Furthermore, a facile asymmetric supercapacitor with a high energy density of 16.5 W $\mathbf{h}\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ was successfully constructed in KOH electrolyte, which uses mercantile active carbon as the negative electrode and Ni-MOF as the positive electrode.[59]A Ni-based MOF with a layered architecture was prepared and served as a pole material for a supercapacitor, which had a high multiplicative capability, large specific capacitance, and cycling stabil­ ity (Fig. 5i) [101].
MOFs have gained increasing attention as potential electrode mate­ rials for SCs. Nevertheless, the low conductivity of most MOFs hinders their capacitive and/or rate properties to a large extent. An improved process to reduce the bulk resistances of MOFs by electrochemically intertwining polyaniline (PANI) with MOF crystal chains around MOFs was introduced. Specifically, Co-based MOF crystals on carbon cloth (CC) were prepared, and further electrodeposited PANI was used to construct an agile conductive porous electrode without changing the underlying architecture of the MOF substrate. Electrochemical studies showed that PANI-ZIF-67-CC had a good areal capacitance of $2146~\mathrm{mF}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $10\mathrm{mVs}^{-1}$ . An agile solid-state SC based on PANI-ZIF-67-CC was also assembled and tested. This progress may have important implica­ tions for designing novel MOF-based SCs and other electrochemical devices. [102] Ce-MOF/CNT and Ce-MOF/GO were prepared using a hygric chemical strategy, and their porosities, crystal properties, morphological characteristics, and functional groups were investigated using diverse techniques. The Ce-MOF in the composites, which were obtained by this approach, exhibited a nanorod architecture of ${\approx}150$ nm. The electrochemical properties of the composites were studied in 3 M KOH and a mixture of 3 M KOH and 0. $2\mathrm{~M~K_{3}F e}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}$ . At a current density of $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , the Ce-MOF/GO composite showed an energy density of 111.05 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ and a maximum specific capacitance of 2221.2 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ; thus, the electrochemical performance of the Ce-MOF/GO com­ posite was enhanced in both electrolytes. The large mesoporous archi­ tecture of Ce-MOF/GO and the presence of oxygen functional groups could promote ion migration in the electrolyte/electrode interphase. Thus, the study results indicated that the composite could act as a highperformance SC electrode material [103].
## 4.2. LIBs
As shown in Fig. 6a, LIBs are nearly 60 years old. In recent years, rechargeable LIBs, owing to their extraordinary cycling properties, light weight, high energy density, and environmental friendliness, have received widespread attention as potential power sources and are widely used in hybrid electric vehicles (HEVs), EES, and medical devices (Fig. 6c) [118,119]. Nanowires are favored for their universal structural compatibility, fast transport kinetics and good flexibility. Recent studies have indicated that 1D nanomaterials have better capacitance and electrochemical activity than their bulk counterparts. Minimizing MOF bulk to the nanoscale is of great importance for deepening their use in sensing, catalysis, energy storage, and so on. MOF nanostructures, especially nanowires, are highly desirable because they have good flexibility, common structural compatibility, and fast transport kinetics. Hollow architectures allow electrode materials to improve their lithium storage properties with respect to cycling stability, rate capability, and specific capacity [120]. Above all, the hollow cavity can provide free strain relaxation, capacitive space, and volume-change accommodation for electrode materials during reiterated lithium inter­ calation–deintercalation. The hollow architecture is also usually asso­ ciated with a high surface area, which can offer additional active sites for lithium storage, reducing the chalking of active materials and significantly improving the cycling performance. Finally, the permeable and thin shells of the hollow architectures can alleviate the diffusion distances of both electrons and lithium ions, thus enhancing the rate capability of the latter [121,122].
Conventional LIBs do not meet the desires of energy density and high power density. Though the three essential components of the battery that are the electrolyte, anode and cathode, have been extensively dis­ cussed, few of them have been modified to meet the effective needs of EES. In order to go beyond the current level of LIB technology, novel and redesigned electrode materials need to be developed, and Li recharge­ able batteries must have a long cycle life, and perform chemical and physical adaptability. In conventional Li-ion batteries, graphite is used as the anode material with capacities as low as $372\mathrm{mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and poor rate performance. As a novel crystalline material, MOFs are fascinating LIB electrode materials owing to their ordered arrangement of metal active sites and crystalline porous architectures. To date, micro/nano­ structured MOFs have been extensively investigated as anode and cathode materials for LIBs [123,124]. A 1D MOF nanowire was proposed as an example of a cobalt polycarboxylate coordination polymer (CoCOP), prepared using a scalable and facile approach. The CoCOP nanowires supply a Li-ion storage capacity exceeding $1100\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at $20\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is impressive. In addition to their high power and energy performance $(6422\mathsf{W}\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ and $875\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ , which are similar to those of recent advanced metal oxide hybrid electrodes, they display an amazing long-range cycle constancy of more than 1000 cycles for completely reversible lithiation–delithiation chemical reactions, which are based on the combined mechanism of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C o}}$ conversion reactions and intercalated architectural deformation. Typical full batteries with commercial $\mathrm{LiFePO}_{4}$ cathodes and CoCOP nanowire anodes (abbrevi­ ated $\mathsf{C o C O P-L i F e P O_{4}}$ ) have superior cycling performance and rapid charge–discharge capability with an $83\%$ capacity preservation rate and beyond 300 cycles at a rate of 1C (1C is equivalent to an hour of discharge). In addition, the high specific energy of $222\mathrm{~W~h~kg}^{-1}$ and $138~\mathrm{mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at 0.1C (in terms of the gross weight of $\mathrm{LiFePO}_{4}$ and CoCOP) for a large capacity also demonstrates their superior perfor­ mance compared to conversion devices and typical commercial energy storage [125]. A new class of Co-based MOFs was synthesized by the hydrothermal process of assembling ${\mathsf{C o}}(\mathrm{II})$ with $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ (Co-BTC MOF) for the first time. These Co-BTC MOFs have a creep-serrated morphology with a width of $55\mathrm{nm}$ and length of $126~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Co-BTC MOFs, which are treated without incineration, are used as LIB anode materials and exhibit excellent electrochemical properties, which include superior coulombic efficiency (over $99\%$ in the 199th cycle), high lithium storage capacity $\mathrm{1739~mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ during the first discharge process), and cycling ability (approximately $4750~\mathrm{\mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ above 200 cycles) within the promising range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ This extraordinary electrochemical property can be attributed to the conjugated carboxylate acid and the linker architecture. More impor­ tantly, the facile synthesis route can be extended to include other metalbased MOFs. In addition, these interesting results suggest that nanosized MOFs will be considered as advanced anodes for lithium storage in the future [126].
MOF derivatization has proven to be an efficient process for achieving porous metal oxides with improved electrochemical proper­ ties and controlled morphological characteristics for Li batteries. A 1D mesoporous NiO nanorod was prepared by a facile and rapid roomtemperature reaction for the first time, using a rod-shaped Ni-MOF precursor as the raw material. The mesoporous NiO nanorod displays a reversible capacity greater than the theoretically calculated $1019\mathrm{mAl}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ over 100 cycles at $100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , has high multiplicity capabilities, and can also act as an anode material for rechargeable Li batteries. The morphological characteristics of the 1D nanoparticle-integrated nano­ rod, along with its mesoporous architecture derived from the MOF precursor, can promote lithium diffusion, maintain the architectural stability of the electrode, and prevent volume transformation during repetitive cycling, thus contributing to enhanced cyclability and reversible Li-storage capacities [127]. The convertible charging of Co1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (Co-BDC) MOF, synthesized through a onepot solvothermal process, was investigated for use as an LIB anode. The MOF anode was found to offer high reversible capacities (the reversible capacities at current densities of 0.2 and 1 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ were 1090 and $611\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ , respectively) and an impressive rate performance. Such an extraordinary Li-ion storage performance for LIB anodes in the MOF class has not been reported before. IR spectroscopy and ex-situ Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) studies of the material in diverse charge states indicate that cobalt maintains the $+2$ state during charging and discharging, in which case ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ can be injected into the organic groups without directly involving the cobalt ions [68].
A method for the successful synthesis of a succession of 1D Mobased/carbon composites with different nanoarchitectures, which uses MOFs as a medium, was demonstrated. The process begins with exqui­ sitely designed $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ nanorods, and controlled crystal growth results in a layer of MOFs on the surface of $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ , forming a distinct core shell architecture. Then, by directly carbonizing at certain temperatures, the precursors can be converted in situ into $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ or $\mathbf{MoO}_{2}$ , which are both well restricted in conductive porous carbons, where the MOF shell acts as both the reagent for the reaction with $\mathbf{MoO_{3}}$ and as a carbon source. All the optimized products showed enhanced electrochemical properties as a result of the design; when evaluated as anode materials for LIBs, especially the core shell $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}/\mathtt{C}$ and hollow $\mathsf{M o o}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ electrodes, they exhibited the best reversible capacities of 810 and $530\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ , even at a current density of $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ for 600 cycles. Therefore, this research extends the application of MOFs, which are a class of covered materials, and exploits the attractive Li storage properties of the Mo-based com­ pounds [128].
## 4.3. Lithium– $O_{2}$ batteries
MOFs have outstanding potential to ameliorate the electrochemical properties of $\mathrm{Li}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries with high $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ accessibility and catalytic activity of the open metal sites. Although it is still in its infancy, the Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}{\bf{O}}_{2}$ cell is a promising technology for energy storage (Fig. 6d). In practical applications, it is recommended that number of cycles in ambient air for $\mathrm{Li}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries is 100 times $\phantom{0}\cdot\sim78$ days) with a discharge capacity of $2000\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ . The development of a solid Li-ion conductor rather than a decomposable liquid electrolyte is a good option, but also a challenge that can also act as both a Li protector and a cathode electrolyte. MOF has a accessible metal location, high surface area and flexible architec­ ture, making it an excellent catalyst for improving the cycling properties of $\mathrm{Li}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries. A new mixed-matrix membrane (MMM) was synthe­ sized by introducing polydopamine-coated CAU-1- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF crystals into a poly(methyl methacrylate) matrix. The MMM has the advantages of high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture capability, high $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ permeability, and extraordinary hydrophobic properties. The MMM was assembled into Li–air batteries, which exhibited potential electrochemical properties in ambient air with a relative humidity of $30\%$ [129].
Rechargeable nanoqueous Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}_{}\mathrm{{O_{2}}}$ batteries are considered to be one of the most promising alternative energy storage devices. MOF nano­ materials with different sizes and morphological characteristics were successfully prepared through a simple solvothermal process. The size of Co-MOF-74 could be reduced to only a few micrometers by employing modulators in an admixed solvent system. In addition, high specific capacities were obtained when they were used as positive pole materials for $\mathrm{Li}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ cells. In addition to the high density of unsaturated electro­ chemically active sites offered by the skeleton itself, the inherent defects and the size limitation of the nanocrystals further provided effective diffusion paths and reduced transmission barriers, leading to their good properties in $\mathrm{Li}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries [130].
The $\mathrm{Li}{\cdot}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ batteries are potential candidates for high-performance energy-storage systems owing to their enormous energy density, which greatly exceeds that of traditional LIBs; the agglomeration and low electrical conductivity of cobalt oxide $(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ can weaken the electrochemical properties. A simple post-thermal treatment process for preparing porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ /carbon composite materials derived from MOFs, which served as the negative pole in rechargeable Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}_{}{\bf{0}}_{2}$ cells, was presented. The use of cobalt-containing MOFs as a sacrificial shuttering could produce equal spreading of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanogranules in the carbon matrix, which ameliorated the issues encountered when employing only $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ as the cathode material. The synthesized porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ /carbon composites exhibited excellent electrochemical properties, such as a high reversible capacity and a low overpotential of ${\approx}9850\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ g^{-1}$ at a current density of $100\mathrm{\mAg}^{-1}$ . They also showed superior cyclability up to the $320\mathrm{th}$ cycle, with a finite capacity of $500~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $200\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ . The performance improvement was due to the mesoporous architecture and catalytic activity of the equally distributed $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles in the carbon matrix [131].
## 4.4. LSBs
Li-S batteries are considered to be one of the most promising rechargeable batteries for the next stage [77,132]. Owing to their low cost, superior energy density, and environmental friendliness, Li-S rechargeable batteries are very attractive for electric transportation. The Li − S battery has a specific energy density and a very high theo­ retical capacity, but its low coulombic efficiency and rapid capacity fading have hindered its application (Fig. 6e). Although Li-S batteries have the advantage of high energy density, their long-range stability is poor owing to the dissolution of polysulfides (PSs) during the battery cycling process [47]. LSBs remain difficult to commercialize owing to inherent defects containing (1) the insulating nature of the active cathode material, (2) the formation of lithium arborescent crystal at the anode, and (3) the high solubility of polysulfides. In order to solve these problems, key strategies include the use of micro/nanostructured ma­ terials to improve conductivity and the encapsulation of sulfur in the host substrate. MOFs have been proven to be effective cathode materials for Li-S batteries; however, the influence of sulfur–host interactions in these porous materials are unknown. Li-S batteries are potentially useful for powering flexible devices, and they can effectively solve the problem of shuttle effect insulation and volume expansion of sulfur electrodes. It is indicated that the MOF grain size influences the initial discharge ca­ pacity of the cell and sulfur utilization, while the MOF pore size impacts the percent conversion of polysulfides, implying that a well-designed pore size should preserve polysulfides in the host and facilitate the diffusion of lithium ions. Nanoarchitectures are able to remarkably enhance their property in LIBs [133,134].
The dissolution of PS intermediates during the discharge process is a block that must be surmounted in order to realize long cycle life in the Li S batteries. MOFs are crystalline, nanoporous substances with very high surface areas, and their composition and architecture can be widely varied. MOFs are potential cathode support materials owing to their encapsulation ability, which is provided through MOF pores that can be augmented via chemical adsorption of PS onto coordinately unsaturated (CUS) metal sites. Through experiments, Ni2(dobdc) has been synthe­ sized; thus, this MOF is considered a potential cathode support for Li − S batteries [135]. A strategy for using foldable interpenetrated MOF/ carbon nanotube films for binderless advanced Li-S batteries through a simple restrained transformation is presented. The carbon nanotubes permeate each other via the MOF crystal and intertwine the electrode into a laminar architecture to offer both structural and conductivity integrity. However, highly porous MOFs impart strong sulfur confine­ ment to the electrodes for good cyclability [136].
HKUST-1 is used as the main material to trap sulfur and thus reduce the dissolution problem. Large quantities of sulfur $(40~\mathrm{wt\%})$ have been added to the HKUST-1 pore metrics, leading to a compound HKUST- $1\supset$ S composite. The strong restriction of HKUST-1 for sulfur is attributed to the open $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ sites and the suitable pore interfaces, which endows the $\mathrm{Li}-\mathsf{S}\subset\mathrm{HKSUT}\cdot1$ battery with extraordinary properties with a capacity of approximately $500~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ over 170 cycles [137]. A method of sulfur impregnation with mesoporous chromium triformate MOF MIL$100(\mathrm{Cr})$ as the substrate substance was reported. It was discovered that sulfur-containing electrodes impregnated within the MOF pores significantly improved the capacity retention of the Li-S cathodes. XPS measurements and complementary TEM studies showed that the PSs were reversibly released and trapped through the pores of MOF during cycling, indicating a knot between the oxygenated framework and PSs. Apart from the pore sizes, the surface activity of the mesoporous addi­ tives also had a significant effect on the cycling properties of MOF [138].
In LIBs, the granule size of the electrode material is considered to be a crucial factor that determines the electrochemical properties. Among LiS batteries, multi-hole substances are mainly used to contain sulfur and inhibit the effusion of PSs, while the granule size of the host remains largely unexplored as a crucial parameter. ZIF-8, a MOF with proven potential for sulfur storage, was chosen as the proof-of-concept design, and five sets of ZIF-8 specimens with diverse granule sizes (from less than $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ to greater than $1~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ were systematically employed as composite ${\mathsf{S@M O F}}$ cathodes. The results indicate that sulfur availability increases monotonically with the decrease in the ZIF-8 granule size (less than $20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ : greater than $950\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ at 0.5C), yet the even better result is the enhanced cycling stability ( $75\%$ after 250 cycles at 0.5C), which is obtained with a medium particle size (approximately $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ [139]. $s@Z I F-8$ , a sulfur and MOF ZIF-8 composite material, was com­ pounded via the melt-diffusion process and employed as a cathode for Li-S batteries. The sulfur molecules were wrapped in the ZIF-8 matrix and confined to a small-sized pore cage, which alleviated the dissolution problem during charging and discharging. As a result of the restrictions of the ZIF-8 matrix on the internal sulfur molecules, the Li–S@ZIF-8 battery exhibits an inverted capacity of $\approx510\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ over 100 cycles at a 0.1C discharge/charge rate, demonstrating that $\boldsymbol{s@\mathrm{MOF}}$ materials are ideal candidates for new Li-S battery cathodes [140]. A new approach to improving the electrical conductivities of MOFs in five to seven steps was reported, which fully reveals their potential in electro­ chemical applications. This approach unites the polar and porous nature of MOFs with the electric properties of electric polymers (in this case, polypyrrole (ppy)) to prepare ppy-MOF compartments for the restriction of sulfur in the Li-S batteries. The ppy-S-in-MOF electrodes outperform those of their ppy and MOF counterparts, particularly at high charging and discharging rates. The pivotal role of ion proliferation in the high rate performance was demonstrated for the first time by comparing ppyMOF compartments with diverse pore architectures. The performance of the ppy-S-in-PCN-224 electrode with cross-linking tunnels and pores was outstanding, with high capacities of 440 and $670\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at 10.0C over 200 and 1000 cycles, respectively, which represents a novel benchmark for long-periodic properties at a high rate in the Li-S batte­ ries [141].
Unlike an embedded negative pole, which has a constitutional host architecture consisting of redox-active metal sites that allow the trans­ portation of $\mathrm{Li^{+}/e^{-}}$ , sulfur, as an alternative negative pole, demands an extra host to place and fix the mobile redox cores, which are PSs. MOFs are a good platform for investigating efficient hosts with reasonable deviation. By choosing the appropriate electrolyte and cut-off voltage, sulfur, which is contained in a suitable MOF host, has the ability to act as a negative pole for both conversion (high energy density) and interca­ lation (stable and fast). In another study, a cathode with a long cycle life based on ZIF-8 nanocrystals and sulfur was presented. When the elec­ trode contained $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ sulfur, it exhibited superior discharge capabil­ ities of $1055\mathrm{mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ (sulfur-based) under 0.1C and almost $710\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1C. After 300 cycles at 0.5C, the decay rate was $0.08\%$ for each cycle, which was particularly evident in long-cycle LSBs. By comparing with other diverse MOFs, such as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53 (Al), HKUST-1, and MIL53 (Al), and both sets of ZIF-8 with granule sizes in the micrometer range, it can be inferred that the tiny granule size of the MOF host can realize small apertures and a high capacity when linked with function­ alities with an affinity for PS anions in an open framework that can assist in achieving stable cycling. These results are generalizable and appli­ cable for the development of novel hosts for other porous substance families of sulfur to engender a greater number of stable and efficient LiS batteries [142]. The Li-S battery is one of the most promising energy storage devices owing to its high specific capacity, which is $1675\mathrm{\mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ . Nevertheless, a major challenge to overcome prior to practical ap­ plications is its fast capacity degradation, which is mainly caused by PS dissolution. Zheng’s work proves that a new Ni-based MOF can immo­ bilize PSs in the cathode architecture via chemical and physical in­ teractions at the molecular level. A capacity retention of $89\%$ was achieved after 100 cycles at 0.1C [143].
## 4.5. Other batteries
An effective bifunctional catalyst was prepared by forming a conductive and interlinked Co-N/C framework on hollow MnO nano­ wires, in a shape similar to bamboo. This hybrid material has a high latent capacity in rechargeable Zn–air batteries and prominent ORR/ OER activity. In particular, stable discharge–charge cycling platforms and high open-circuit potential were achieved when they were assem­ bled into solid-phase Zn–air cells. The synergistic effect of these two composites in 1D nanowires promotes a continuous and uniform spread of the electrolyte to the active sites. Novel guidelines were provided for optimizing materials derived from MOFs as candidates for $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pt/}$ C precious-metal catalysts using air as a negative pole in liquid- and solid-state Zn–air cells [155,156]. Alkaline battery–supercapacitor hybrid devices (ABSHDs) have received considerable attention owing to their combined advantages of both SCs and alkaline batteries. A Ni-MOF with high specific capacity and improved energy storage could be further enhanced using a novel strategy that harnessed the synergy be­ tween $\mathrm{Fe(CN)_{6}}^{4-}/\mathrm{Fe(CN)_{6}}^{3-}$ and Ni-MOF. The special stratiform crystal architecture of the Ni-MOF offers ample interspace for $\mathrm{Fe(CN)_{6}}^{3-}/\mathrm{Fe}$ $\left(\mathrm{CN}\right)_{6}{}^{4-}$ diffusion and storage, and $\mathrm{Fe(CN)_{6}}^{3-}\mathrm{/Fe(CN)_{6}}^{4-}$ could be used as an electronic relay during discharge–charge transversions by coupling $\mathrm{{Ni(III)}/N i(I I)}$ in the Ni-MOF electrode. In addition, an Ni-MOF in an electrolyte composed of 0.1 M ${\mathrm{K}}_{4}{\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}$ and $3\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH possesses an expanded voltage window of $1.4\mathrm{V}$ that led to both a high power density $(7000~\mathrm{W~kg}^{-1})$ and high energy density $(55.8\mathrm{~W~h~kg}^{-1})$ . Thus, the re­ sults of this study could offer insightful guidelines for broadening the applications of MOFs in energy storage devices and exploring other
redox additives [116] (Table 2).
## 5. 1D MOFs for electrochemical energy conversion
Composites can be synthesized from multi-walled carbon nanotubes and cobalt-porphyrin-based MOFs, which possess excellent catalytic activity toward OER, ORR, and the HER. This also satisfies the urgent demand for sustainable and clean conversion technologies and energy storage. MOFs have a highly porous architecture and high specific sur­ face area that can offer enough metal active sites for electrocatalytic reactions with rapid mass transfer and excellent electron transfer [11,94,158].
## 5.1. 1D MOFs for electrocatalysis
The search for cost-effective OER and ORR electrocatalysts as sub­ stitutes for those containing precious metals has great implications for energy storage devices [155]. It should be pointed out that several rare/ precious metals and their oxides are able to serve as classical catalysts and electrode materials, yet these have various issues such as scarcity, high cost and low durability. In general, homogeneous molecular cata­ lysts have the advantages of permanence, easy architectural design, high activity and the possibility of mechanistic studies. However, it is hard to control the number and location of active sites on the surface of solid catalysts and the reaction circumstance, causing poor stability and reproducibility of MOFs. MOFs are excellent candidates for electro­ catalysts due to their distinct advantages of tunable crystalline porous architectures and onefold metal sites divided by linkers. MOFs can perform the connatural advantages of both homogeneous and hetero­ geneous catalysts [159,160]. The ORR, OER, and HER play key roles in the development of sustainable and clean energy systems. In terms of these reactions, efficient electrocatalysts are highly demanded not only to reduce their overpotentials but also to facilitate the practical appli­ cations of related energy installations [161]. The $\mathrm{Co-NC}\ @\cdot\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ catalyst exhibits extraordinary OER and HER performance, owing to its distinct synergetic effects and reverse-encapsulation architecture of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ and Co-NC. In alkaline solution, it provides a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ at a low overpotential of $347~\mathrm{mV}$ for OER and $99~\mathrm{mV}$ for HER. It is interesting to note that the catalyst contributes to stability in acidic solutions and extraordinary HER catalytic activity (at a low over­ potential of $143~\mathrm{mV}.$ ), owing to the protection of $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ by the reversed encapsulation architecture of the Co nanoparticles [162].
## 5.1.1. OER
MOFs are a potential category of multiple heterogeneous catalytic materials, but they are seldom used directly for OE electrocatalysis. The use of MOFs as shuttering for electrocatalysts in situ through backout has been the focus of some research.
It remains a challenging task to find a nonprecious-metal bifunc­ tional electrocatalyst suitable for both HER and OER with superior durability and higher efficiency than the benchmark precious-metalbased electrocatalysts. To solve this problem, $\mathrm{FeNi_{3}–F e_{3}O_{4}}$ NPs/MOTCNT and MOF nanosheets were designed and synthesized via a simple hydrothermal reaction followed by the partial decomposition of earthabundant and low-cost $_\mathrm{Ni/Fe/C}$ precursors. Owing to its distinct porous nanostructure fabricated by ultrafine nanoparticles attached to 1D CNT matrix/ 2D nanosheets, it could serve as a bifunctional elec­ trocatalyst with excellent electrocatalytic hydrolysis activity, providing a small Tafel slope of $37\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ for the OER with a low overpotential of $234\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve an activity of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ ; the activity can reach $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for HER at an overpotential of as low as $108~\mathrm{{mV}}$ and an overpotential as low as $360\mathrm{mV}$ , which is superior to that of noble metalbased electrocatalysts $393\mathrm{mV}$ for $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{n}}_{10}$ and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\mathrm{.}$ ). In addition, the electrocatalyst exhibited superior long-term stability. A reasonable nanostructure design and simple preparation process have been pro­ posed to achieve efficient, long-lasting, and excellent performance of nonprecious-metal-based electrocatalysts [163].
A variety of Ni/Fe-based trimetallic MOFs have been synthesized using a solvothermal method and can be immediately employed as highefficiency electrocatalysts. These MOFs exhibit huge OER activities as a constituent function. The optimized Ni/Fe-based MOF can obtain a current density of $20\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ at a low overpotential of $236\mathrm{mV}$ , and the Tafel slope was $52.2~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, direct growth on nickel foam (NF) can further advance the OER properties of these MOFs. [69] MOFs have also become increasingly attractive for solar energy appli­ cations. A mixed-node MOF catalyst, $\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}{-}74$ $(0<\mathbf{x}\leq1\dot{}\end{array}$ ) serves as an effective electrocatalyst for OER in alkaline media with a small Tafel slope $(56~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1})$ , very low overpotential ( $280~\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , and high induction efficiency $(91\%)$ , and can deliver a current density of $20\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ at $1.58{\mathrm{~V}}$ for overall water splitting. In addition, by combining multiple spectroscopic methods, such as electron spin resonance, X-ray absorption, and XPS, the mechanism behind the improved catalysis of $\mathsf{C o}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Fe}_{1-\mathrm{x}^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}{-}74$ , which was compared to its single-metal counterparts, was revealed. The admixed-node MOF offers an enhanced electron-rich environment and more open metal sites, which contribute to impactful charge trans­ formation and significantly enhance OER activity [164].
## 5.1.2. ORR
It is very important to improve the efficiency of electrocatalysts for conversion technologies and EESs, such as metal–air batteries and fuel cells. The development of novel OR electrocatalysts to replace rare and expensive platinum-based catalysts is a significant focus of conversion and energy storage research. Conductive and porous MOFs are consid­ ered prospective materials for the ORR owing to their high surface area and well-developed pores, as well as their versatile structural features and chemical compositions [165–168].
High-performance electrocatalysts for the ORR are essential in a great deal of electrochemical conversion and energy storage technolo­ gies. Nevertheless, the shortage of inexpensive and efficient catalysts or catalyst systems that have the ability to compete with noble metal cat­ alysts has hindered their extensive industrial application. MOFs with customized components and architectures have recently been consid­ ered as potential precursors for a variety of functional materials. A dualMOF constrained-pyrolysis method for the fabrication of nested carbon nanotube assemblies with iron carbide nanoparticles via double-MOFconstrained pyrolysis was reported. Starting from a structurally com­ plex composite material based on a Zn-based MOF polyhedral matrix and a new MOF-in-MOF precursor with multi-encapsulated iron-based MOF nanorods, a catalyst with carbon nanotubes encapsulated with iron carbide nanocrystals in a porous carbon matrix was successfully pre­ pared. The as-derived composite exhibited remarkable electrocatalytic properties for the ORR in an alkaline electrolyte. This strategy represents a new tool for the synthesis of MOF-derived functional materials and promises extensive applications [169]. Heteroatom-mixed carbon sub­ stances have been deemed as one of the most promising substitutes for Pt-based catalysts. A double-doped nitrogen–sulfur 1D carbon rod catalyst, which has porous graphene-like layers that are derived from Nibased MOFs on the surface, has been exploited. The Ni-NSPC-6 catalyst has a considerable specific surface area and porous architecture with a uniform spread of S and N atoms in the carbon skeleton. Owing to its unusual 1D morphological characteristics, the synergistic effect of the uniform co-doping of N and S, the convenient channel offered by the porous architecture, and the existence of an appropriate amount of $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2},$ the Ni-NSPC-6 catalyst exhibits extraordinary electrocatalytic properties for the ORR, including half-wave potentials and positive onset, a low Tafel slope, high catalytic current density, and extraordi­ nary robustness. The customizable construction of MOF-derived codoped N and S carbon materials with porous nanoarchitectures is promising for the development of high-performance electrocatalysts [170].
Well-defined carbon-based structures were designed and fabricated by the orientated growth of MOF arrays and in situ nucleation on the interface of LDH nanosheets, which were subsequently subjected to pyrolysis and showed significantly improved electrocatalytic ORR properties. The prepared MOF arrays were distinctly and strongly attached to the segmented interface of LDHs, ensuring the architectural transition of MOFs to carbon-based arrays with meticulous control over morphological characteristics and composition. The obtained carbonbased network exhibited ideal characteristics, such as a surface area of $220\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , highly efficient active sites, and unique mesoporous/microarchitecture (primarily concentrated at $1.5{-}3~\mathrm{nm}$ and $10{-}20~\mathrm{\nm}^{\cdot}$ ). As a result, the electrocatalyst had superior ORR activity and stability (0.94 V onset potential and current retention time of over $20,000\ s$ (or ${\approx}99\%$ ) compared to RHE), making it superior to the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ material [171]. Ultrathin $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{N i}$ thermometal organic framework nanobelts have been produced using a hydrothermal process to serve as high-efficiency electrocatalysts. The optimized ultrathin Ni/Co nanobelts can obtain an initial potential of 0.939 V (close to $\scriptstyle0.940\mathrm{\V}$ , which corresponds to that of $\mathrm{Pt/C)}$ , and $\mathrm{Ni/Co}$ is superior to the $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ in terms of stability toward ORR. In addition, at a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , the ultrathin Co/ Ni nanoribbons exhibit an electric potential of $1.478\mathrm{~V~}$ for the OER, which is better than that of $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ , revealing the great latent capacity of MOFs as bifunctional oxygen catalysts in metal–air cells. Moreover, the ultrathin Ni/Co nanoribbon samples are lighter in color and can also be captured by the channel that blurs but still exists following ORR and OER. The IR spectroscopy, XPS, and XRD results for the ultrathin Ni/Co nanobelts before and after catalysis are essentially consistent and indi­ cate that the synergistic effect between Co and Ni along with the ul­ trathin belt-like architecture are the keys to regulating the electrocatalytic activity [58].
## 5.1.3. HER
The development of impactful earth-abundant electrocatalysts over a wide pH range is critical for the practical use of the HER; however, this endeavor remains challenging.
MOF-derived MOF- $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ prepared with $\mathsf{C o s e}_{2}$ nanoparticles anchored into N-doped graphitic carbon was fabricated via in situ selenization of Co-based MOFs. The obtained MOF- $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ shows ideal HER properties: it exhibits a small Tafel slope of $42\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ and a low onset potential of $150~\mathrm{mV}$ and provides excellent long-range stability and a high current density. This enhancement is due to the distinctive Ndoped MOF-derived structure with high conductivity, which not only provides a sufficient amount of active reaction sites but also ensures a stable connection between the Ni-doped carbon matrix and the $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ nanoparticles, contributing to the extraordinary electrocatalytic activity for HER [172].
## 5.2. 1D MOFs for photocatalysis
1D MOF s have a highly porous architecture and high specific surface area that can offer enough metal active sites for photocatalysis. Jiang et al. recently demonstrated the design of a multifunctional MOF-based heterojunction architecture consisting of 1D MIL-53(Fe) microrods and magnetic nanosphere embellishment (MHMCs), which can be simply synthesized via a one-pot solvothermal reaction method. As a photo­ anode, the MHMCs provide a stable, distinct, and repeatable photocur­ rent response and show effective photoelectrochemical properties for water oxidation under visible light (Fig. 7a–b). In addition, the MHMCs were highly effective in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -mediated visible-light photocatalysis process. The rate constants for the degradation of PNP and RhB by the $\mathrm{MHMCs/H_{2}O_{2}}$ system were higher than those of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}}$ pho­ tocatalysts under visible-light illumination. A novel perspective for the reasonable deviation of advanced MOF-based materials for energy use and environmental applications was provided. [67] Ghaddar et al. re­ ported the successful binding of the photoactive $\mathrm{{Ru}(c p t p y)_{2}}$ strut into a stable MOF, AUBM-4. Single-crystal XRD confirmed the synthesis of a novel 1D $\mathrm{{Ru}(c p t p y)_{2}}$ complex consisting of eight $z\mathrm{r}$ atoms ligated to O atoms. The chemically stable MOF architecture was used as an effective photocatalyst for the transformation of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to formate under visiblelight irradiation. The achieved transformation rate is the highest in the literature to date for analogous systems (Fig. 7c) [176,177].
Photocatalysis is an effective strategy for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ degradation, in which $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is commonly employed as a catalyst. Conversely, the low adsorp­ tion ability of the gas and the low separation rate of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ photoinduced carriers limit the rate of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ photocatalytic decomposition. Singlecrystalline $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanowires were assembled on 1D CNFs, and a tunable MOF coating was constructed on the surface of the nanowires by employing a generalized one-step self-assembly strategy (Fig. 7d–e). The superior photocatalytic performance of the obtained membrane was attributed to the high and regenerable $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ adsorption ability and the ability of the CNFs to swiftly transport charge carriers of the MOF. The photocatalytic diagram of the prepared material is also shown in Fig. 7f.
Accordingly, a promising approach to improve the photocatalytic per­ formance of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ degradation was proposed [174]. $\upalpha$ -Ferrous oxalate dihydrate ( $\mathrm{{'}}\upalpha$ -FOD), an MOF based on Fe(III) with distinct n-type semi­ conductor morphologies, has been successfully constructed for use as a bifunctional catalyst in both Fenton oxidation and photocatalysis. The photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ to $\mathrm{Cr(III)}$ under visible-light illumi­ nation (5 W LED lamp) indicated that $\upalpha$ -FOD had superior photocatalytic activity, and $100\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ was reduced to $50\mathrm{mL}$ within $6\mathrm{{min}}$ . In addition, $\upalpha$ -FOD showed obvious Fenton oxidation activity in degrading organic chemical compounds in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under dark con­ ditions [175].
## 6. Summary and outlook
Nowadays, with the development of nanotechnology, nanostructures have become a research focus. MOFs, as a novel category of porous crystalline materials, have received considerable attention owing to their high surface areas, tunable architecture, and permanent porosity. As a growing class of multiphase catalysts, MOF-based materials, which are rationally designed and constructed (Fig. 8a), are equipped with various catalytic centers and have a broad range of catalytic applica­ tions, including energy storage, catalysis, sensors, and conversion. In general, this review offers an overview of the breakthroughs in the development of 1D MOFs for electrochemical applications, including batteries, SCs, electrocatalysis, and photocatalysis (Fig. 8b). There is no doubt that the dynamic behavior and framework flexibility are distinct characteristics of MOFs (compared with other classes of porous mate­ rials) and substantially contribute to their properties such as thermal expansion and adsorption [180]. Simultaneously, comprehensive syn­ thetic strategies for controlling the morphological characteristics of 1D architectures have been introduced. The use of these MOFs as electrode materials in SCs, rechargeable batteries, and catalysts have also been investigated. Owing to the structural characteristics of highly porous architecture and high specific surface area, MOFs are usually combined with the capacitive characteristics of metal phosphates, good catalytic performance, and good stability and durability. It should be noted that the direct use of MOFs as electrode materials remains in its initial stage and is associated with many challenges, especially the restricted sta­ bility and conductivity of MOFs. Chemical and thermal stability are of considerable importance for electrochemical applications of MOF ma­ terials. However, there are some serious disadvantages that limit their application in electrochemistry. For example, the disadvantage of MOFs is their poor conductivity. Since the charging and discharging cycle life of most MOFs is less than 10,000, he column effect cannot be equal to $100\%$ , thereby shortening the cycle life of MOFs; therefore, it is neces­ sary to overcome the limited cycle life and ensure good circulation. Nanostructures have been extensively used in SCs, batteries, catalysis, and other fields owing to their distinct surface effects, easy size control, and good adhesion with the substrate. Accordingly, it is essential to investigate the electrochemical applications of 1D MOFs and make an account of the recent progress.
The incomplete statistical histogram of the number of articles in 1D MOF by year in recent years shows that 1D MOF has received a great deal of attention (Fig. 8c). MOF-based materials have unique advantages compared to the conventional catalysts. First, high segregation and dispersion of active sites can maximize atom utilization, and tunable and well-distributed pore architectures and sizes facilitate mass transfer and particle size screening. Second, designable and unambiguous crystalline architectures are of great importance for the comprehension of struc­ ture–function relationships and catalytic mechanism studies. Third, the simple functionalization of organic ligands and metal nodes allows for the rational deviation and the presence of multifunctional active sites. Fourth, the easily tailorable chemical surroundings of the active sites facilitate the catalytic activity/selectivity, and an ultrahigh specific surface area promotes the access of substrate concentration and catalytic sites. Finally, the pore space offers innate conditions for the construction of MOF composites by the introduction of guest species. 1D MOFs, compared with bulk MOF crystals, offer the advantages of a high density of surface active sites and distinct flexibility, as well as distinct physi­ cochemical performances. Meanwhile, due to their porosity, the distinct performances of 1D MOFs have attracted lots of attention and have shown good property in various fields. What’s more, MOFs are able to be used as carriers to load catalytically active nanoparticles, and 1D MOFs are capable of serving as building blocks to construct hierarchically architectural materials.
From the above discussion, despite the tremendous progress made in the recent years, it is clear 1D MOFs also have many challenges.
(1) It is important to point out that the direct use of MOFs as elec­ trode materials remains in the initial stage and is confronted with numerous challenges, particularly the restricted stability and conductivity of MOFs. Chemical and thermal stability are important for electrochemical applications of the MOF materials. MOFs exhibit relatively low stability comparable to carbon-based materials, metal oxides, and other electrode materials, particu­ larly in alkaline/acidic aqueous conditions. Conversely, other derived materials and their composites have broad application prospects as electrode materials, although the high production cost and low yield are not conducive to large-scale production for practical applications.
(2) The poor electrical conductivity of MOFs is also an important issue that should be considered. While some conductive MOF materials have been constructed recently, their use as electrodes in SCs, batteries, and electrocatalysis remains far from satisfac­ tory. Hence, the combination of micro/nano MOFs with specific morphological characteristics, such as 1D architectural supports, is a viable strategy that not only promotes rapid electron trans­ port and electrolyte diffusion but also improves the stability and durability. It is believed that the conductivity and stability of MOF electrode materials can be improved by functionalization. Owing to the numerous efforts by researchers, a few novel conductive MOFs have emerged that can significantly accelerate the capability of MOFs to be directly used as electrodes in elec­ trochemical applications. Over the years, the application of conducting MOFs as solid electrolytes in batteries has also appeared, which is a significant advancement in MOF electronic devices.
Although these challenges remain, the rapid development of MOF technology makes such functional materials promising for electro­ chemical applications. Despite the tremendous advances, the develop­ ment of controllable and efficient methods to construct micro/nanoMOFs remains necessary. With the growing number of important con­ tributions geared toward solving these issues and advancing MOF-based materials, we are confident that MOF-based practical applications and industrial production will be realized in the near future.
